<Base
  title="How your Bitcoin actually works — keys, words, and signatures · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="An optional, plain-English look under the hood: why your Bitcoin is really a key, what the seed words are, what a 'wallet' actually is, and how signing proves you hold the key without ever revealing it."
  verified="2026-07-17"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">The big picture · optional</p>
    <h1>How your Bitcoin <span class="accent">actually works</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      You don’t need any of this to hold Bitcoin safely — the how-to guides work whether or not you read it.
      But if you’re the kind of person who likes to know what’s <em>really</em> going on under the hood — what
      the words are, what a “key” is, how “signing” works — here it is, in plain English.
    </p>
    <hr class="lead-sep" />

    <h2>1 · Your Bitcoin is really a key</h2>
    <p>
      Your coins don’t sit inside your wallet the way cash sits in a leather one. Every bitcoin lives on a
      shared public ledger — the blockchain — that everyone in the world can see. What makes a coin
      <em>yours</em> is that you, and only you, hold the secret <strong>key</strong> that can move it.
      Hold the key, hold the coins. Lose the key, lose the coins. There’s no bank to call to reset it,
      because there’s no bank — there’s just the key.
    </p>
    <p class="deeper"><strong>Deeper dive:</strong> <a href="/deep-dive/key-space">see why that key can’t be guessed →</a> — the number of possible keys is so vast it stops feeling like a number.</p>

    <h2>2 · The seed words <em>are</em> that key</h2>
    <p>
      A key is really just an enormous, utterly random number — hopeless to copy down by hand without a
      mistake. So your wallet turns it into something human: a list of
      <strong>12 or 24 ordinary words</strong> (the standard is called
      <a href="/glossary#bip-39">BIP-39</a>). Those words <em>are</em> your key, in a form you can write on
      paper or stamp into metal. That’s why this whole guide is obsessed with backing them up safely: the
      <a href="/glossary#seed-phrase">seed words</a> are the money. Anyone who reads them can take everything;
      anyone who loses them loses everything.
    </p>

    <Diagram name="seed-to-words" caption="Your key is a huge random number. The words are the same key, written so a human can copy it." />
    <p class="deeper"><strong>Deeper dive:</strong> <a href="/deep-dive/seed">watch a seed get created, one step at a time →</a> — a throwaway seed, generated safely in your browser. Or <a href="/deep-dive/dice">roll your own with dice →</a>.</p>

    <h2>3 · A “wallet” is a configuration of keys</h2>
    <p>
      Here’s the part that trips people up: a “wallet” isn’t a physical object, and it isn’t the app on your
      phone. It’s a <strong>configuration of keys</strong> — how many keys exist, and how many of them it
      takes to approve a spend. One key on its own is <em>single-signature</em>. Add a secret word and it’s a
      <em>passphrase</em> wallet. Spread it across several keys where a few must agree, and it’s
      <em>multi-signature</em>. The little hardware device is just a safe <strong>place to keep a key</strong> — the
      wallet is the <em>arrangement</em> of keys itself. That distinction is exactly what
      <a href="/ladder">the ladder</a> is about.
    </p>

    <Diagram name="multisig" caption="One way to arrange keys — 2-of-3 multisig: three keys exist, any two together approve a spend, so no single key is a point of failure." />
    <p class="deeper"><strong>Deeper dive:</strong> <a href="/deep-dive/multisig">tap through a live 2-of-3 wallet →</a> — three real keys, and watch any two approve a spend.</p>

    <h2>4 · Public keys and private keys</h2>
    <p>
      Every key actually comes as a matched pair, and the easiest way to picture them is a mailbox:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <strong>public key</strong> — which your <a href="/glossary#address">address</a> is derived from — is the mail slot.
        You can hand it to anyone and they can drop Bitcoin in. Sharing it can’t hurt you.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>private key</strong> is the only key that opens the box and takes anything out. It never
        leaves your device. You share the address freely; you guard the private key with your life.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The clever bit: the two are mathematically linked, so the whole network can confirm a payment was
      approved by the right private key — <strong>without ever seeing it.</strong>
    </p>

    <Diagram name="keys-mailbox" caption="Public key = the mail slot anyone can drop coins into (your address). Private key = the only thing that opens the box." />
    <p class="deeper"><strong>Deeper dive:</strong> <a href="/deep-dive/addresses">see the four address formats one key produces →</a> — why addresses start with <span class="mono">1</span>, <span class="mono">3</span>, <span class="mono">bc1q</span>, or <span class="mono">bc1p</span>. Or <a href="/deep-dive/hd-tree">watch one seed grow a whole tree of them →</a>.</p>

    <h2>5 · Signing — proving it’s you without showing the key</h2>
    <p>
      So how do you spend without revealing your key? You <strong>sign</strong>. When you approve a payment,
      your device uses the private key to produce a one-of-a-kind <strong>signature</strong> for that exact
      transaction — like a wax seal that only your ring can stamp. Anyone can look at the signature and
      confirm it could only have come from your key, but no one can work <em>backwards</em> from it to the key
      itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      That’s why a hardware wallet can sign a payment on a computer riddled with malware and still be safe:
      the key does its work <em>inside</em> the device and never comes out. You’re not sending your key
      anywhere — you’re sending <em>proof</em>.
    </p>

    <Diagram name="signing" caption="You never send your key. The device signs inside itself and sends out a signature — proof anyone can check, but no one can reverse into the key." />
    <p class="deeper"><strong>Deeper dive:</strong> <a href="/deep-dive/signing">sign a payment yourself →</a> — then try to tamper with it and watch the signature break.</p>

    <div class="callout safe">
      <span class="label">The whole thing in one sentence</span>
      <p style="margin:0">
        Your bitcoin is a <strong>key</strong>; the <strong>words</strong> are a backup of that key; a
        <strong>wallet</strong> is how your keys are arranged; and <strong>signing</strong> proves you hold
        the key without ever revealing it. Everything else in this guide is just how to keep that key safe.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

